)**:\n   - The point \\( P \\) equidistant from all vertices \\( A, B, C, \\) and \\( D \\) is the centroid of the tetrahedron. The centroid of a regular tetrahedron is also the center of the circumscribed sphere and the point where the medians intersect.\n\n2. **Properties of the Centroid \\( P \\)**:\n   - Since \\( P \\) is the centroid, it divides each median in the ratio \\( 1:3 \\) from the vertex to the centroid of the opposite face.\n\n3. **Extend Line \\( \\overrightarrow{AP} \\) to Hit Face \\( BCD \\)**:\n   - Extend \\( \\overrightarrow{AP} \\) to intersect the face \\( BCD \\) at point \\( Q \\). Since \\( P \\) is the centroid, it divides the median \\( AQ \\) in the ratio \\( 1:3 \\).\n\n4. **Calculate Ratio \\( PQ/AQ \\)**:\n   - Let's denote the length of \\( AP \\) as \\( x \\). Since \\( P \\) divides the median \\( AQ \\) in the ratio \\( 1:3 \\), we have:\n     \\[\n     AP = \\frac{1}{4} AQ \\quad \\text{and} \\quad PQ = \\frac{3}{4} AQ\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, the ratio \\( PQ/AQ \\) is:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{PQ}{AQ} = \\frac{\\frac{3}{4} AQ}{AQ} = \\frac{3}{4}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the ratio \\( PQ/AQ \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
